.SS "bool stumpless_has_error (void)"

.PP
True if the last call to a stumpless function encountered an error\&. To get the error itself, use the stumpless_get_error function\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe\fP This function is thread safe\&. No synchronization primitives are used as the returned flag is specific to the thread of execution\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Safe\fP This function is safe to call from signal handlers\&. If you do so, note that if it is called before a stumpless library function in the handler itself the result will not be meaningful\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Safe\fP This function is safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled\&.
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
release v1\&.6\&.0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
True if the last stumpless call had an error, false if not\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void stumpless_perror (const char * prefix)"

.PP
Prints information about the current error to the error stream\&. A prefix can be added to the information if desired\&. If one is provided then it will be followed by a colon character and a space, followed by the error information\&. If it is not provided then the colon and space characters will not be printed\&.
.PP
The message will end with a newline\&.
.PP
If there is not currently an active error message, then nothing will be printed (not even the prefix)\&.
.PP
\fBThread Safety: MT-Safe race:prefix\fP This function is thread safe, of course assuming that prefix is not changed by other threads during execution\&. A lock is used to coordinate accesses to the error stream\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Signal Safety: AS-Unsafe lock\fP This function is not safe to call from signal handlers, as it uses a non-reentrant lock to synchronize access to the error stream\&.
.PP
\fBAsync Cancel Safety: AC-Unsafe lock\fP This function is not safe to call from threads that may be asynchronously cancelled, as the lock used to control access to the error stream may not be released after a cancellation\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIprefix\fP An optional prefix to print in front of the message\&. If this is NULL then it will simply be ignored\&. 
.RE
.PP

